course: chinese-street
lesson: 1
title: Hello and goodbye: the first words at the gate
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your friend Ming speaks Mandarin at home, and you pick it up the way he did as a toddler: by ear, never from a book. We write what you hear in pinyin, with little tone marks showing whether the voice stays high, rises, dips or falls.
explain: The first word you catch is nǐ hǎo (hello, literally «you good»). It sounds like «nee HOW», and because two dipping tones in a row make the first one rise, people really say «ní hǎo».
explain: Just as you say «hiya» to a mate but «good morning» to the head teacher, Mandarin has a cheeky hāi for kids, a polite nín hǎo for Ming's granny, and a quick zǎo! for the morning.
explain: At home time you hear zàijiàn (goodbye, «again see»), bāibāi (bye-bye, borrowed from English), míngtiān jiàn (see you tomorrow) and huítóu jiàn (see you later).
explain: And when Ming's nǎinai asks chī le ma? (have you eaten?), she is not offering dinner — it is an old-style hello, like «all right?».
fact: nǐ hǎo (sounds like «nee HOW», really said «ní hǎo») means hello, literally «you good»; it suits anyone, though friends often just shout your name instead.
fact: nín hǎo («neen HOW») is hello with nín, the respectful «you»; it is for grandparents, teachers and shopkeepers, and between kids it sounds stiff.
fact: hāi, said high and level like English «hi», is borrowed from English and is for kids and young people, not for grandparents.
fact: zǎo («dzow», dipping) is a short «morning!» at the school gate; zǎo also means «early», so nǐ zǎo can mean «morning!» or «you're early».
fact: zǎoshang hǎo («DZOW-shahng HOW») is the fuller good morning, said to teachers and parents, and only until about mid-morning.
fact: wǎn'ān («wahn-AHN», two syllables wǎn and ān) means good night, only at bedtime or at the end of a late phone call, never as «good evening» when you arrive.
fact: zàijiàn («dzye-JYEN», both falling) means goodbye, literally «again see»; it is fine for anyone, and if your voice rises at the end it sounds like a question.
fact: bāibāi («bye-bye», high and level) is borrowed from English and is very common in Chinese families in Britain; to a grandparent or teacher zàijiàn is safer.
fact: míngtiān jiàn («MING-tyen JYEN») means see you tomorrow; míngtiān is tomorrow, so on a Friday people say xīngqīyī jiàn (see you Monday).
fact: huítóu jiàn («HWAY-toe JYEN», literally «turn the head and see») is a friendly «see you later» for the same day, not for teachers.
fact: chī le ma? («CHR luh mah», the ch with the tongue curled back) means «have you eaten?»; grandparents and neighbours use it as a greeting, and the polite answer is chī le, nǐ ne? (I have, and you?); answer «no» and a granny may well start cooking for you.
fact: nǐ qù nǎr? («nee CHYOO nar») means «where are you off to?»; aunties use it as a street hello, so a vague answer like chūqù wánr (going out to play) is fine.
fact: nǎr (where?) dips and nàr (there) falls; only the tone tells the two apart.
exercise: What does nǐ hǎo mean? | Hello | Goodbye | Thank you | Good night
exercise: Why does nǐ hǎo sound like «ní hǎo» when people say it? | Two dipping tones in a row make the first one rise | People are always asking a question when they say it | The first word is always shouted | It is a southern accent only
exercise: What is special about nín in nín hǎo? | It is the respectful «you» for elders | It means «little friend» | It is the word for «good» | It is the plural «you all» for kids
exercise: Who is the cheeky hāi best for? | Kids and young people | Ming's grandad | The head teacher | A shopkeeper you don't know
exercise: Ming shouts zǎo! at the gate at 8:30. What does he mean? | Morning! | Hurry up! | Go away! | See you later!
exercise: When do people say zǎoshang hǎo? | In the morning, until about mid-morning | Only at bedtime, just before you sleep | In the late afternoon | Only on someone's birthday
exercise: When is wǎn'ān the right word? | At bedtime or ending a late call | When you arrive for tea | At the school gate in the morning | When someone sneezes
exercise: What does zàijiàn literally mean? | Again see | Go now | Good night | Hello again
exercise: Where does bāibāi come from? | It is borrowed from English «bye-bye» | It is the word for «eight eight» only | It is an old word from emperors | It is a sound a baby makes when hungry
exercise: Ming says míngtiān jiàn on Monday afternoon. What does he mean? | See you tomorrow | See you in the morning | See you on Friday | See you next year
exercise: What does huítóu jiàn mean? | See you later, the same day | See you some time next week | Turn around and go home | Good night, sleep tight, see you soon
exercise: Ming's nǎinai asks chī le ma? at noon. What is she really doing? | Saying hello, the old-fashioned way | Inviting you to a big family dinner tonight | Checking that you are not too thin | Telling you lunch is late
exercise: A good answer to chī le ma? is | chī le, nǐ ne? | wǎn'ān | bú yào | huítóu jiàn
exercise: An auntie asks nǐ qù nǎr? in the street. What fits? | A vague chūqù wánr (going out to play) | Your full home address and the exact time | Nothing, she is being nosy | zǎoshang hǎo
exercise: What tells nǎr (where?) apart from nàr (there)? | Only the tone: a dip or a fall | The first letter of the word | How loudly you say it to someone | The length of the word
exercise: Why is zàijiàn safer than bāibāi with Ming's grandad? | zàijiàn suits anyone, bāibāi is casual | bāibāi is a rude word in Chinese | Grandads don't know English | zàijiàn means «I love you very much, Grandad»
test: «nee HOW means how are you, like english how» Which is right? | No, nǐ hǎo is hello, literally «you good» | Yes, hǎo is just the English word «how» | Yes, it asks how you came here | No, it means goodbye
test: You arrive at Ming's for a sleepover at 7 in the evening and say wǎn'ān to his mum. What went wrong? | wǎn'ān is good night, for bedtime, not a hello | Nothing, wǎn'ān is the usual good evening | wǎn'ān is only for babies | wǎn'ān means «I'm hungry»
test: What would happen if you said a big «hāi!» to Ming's grandad? | It would sound a bit too cheeky; nín hǎo makes him beam | He would not understand any English at all | It means «go away» in Chinese | Nothing, hāi is the most polite hello
test: «ming said nǐ zǎo to me and i was already late lol» What could nǐ zǎo also mean? | You're early (or just morning!) | You're very late again | You're tired | You're in big trouble
test: It is Friday home time. Which is better than míngtiān jiàn? | xīngqīyī jiàn (see you Monday) | wǎn'ān | zǎoshang hǎo | huítóu jiàn lǎoshī (see you later)
test: You say zàijiàn with your voice going up at the end. How does it sound? | Like a question, «goodbye?» | Like a very polite goodbye | Like «good morning» | Like you are cross with them
test: «his nan asked if i ate, i said no and now theres like 6 dishes» Why? | chī le ma is a greeting, but a «no» makes grannies feed you | She thought you were rude | It is a test of good manners at school | Saying no means you want to stay overnight at their house
test: A cartoon kid says zǎoshang hǎo at 4 in the afternoon. What would a real kid say? | nǐ hǎo | wǎn'ān | zǎo | míngtiān jiàn
test: «shut up, it's ní hǎo, not nǐ hǎo, you're saying it wrong» Who is right? | Both: it is written nǐ, but said rising before hǎo | Only the grumpy kid; nǐ does not exist | Nobody; the word is nīn hǎo | Only you; nǐ never ever changes, whatever comes next
test: Ming and you will meet again at the park after tea. What do you say at the gate? | huítóu jiàn | wǎn'ān | zǎoshang hǎo | nín hǎo
test: In a game a character greets the emperor with «hāi, bāibāi!». What is off? | Both are casual English loans, and one of them is goodbye | Nothing, both are formal | hāi means «long live» | bāibāi is the hello for emperors
exam: Identify the greeting most appropriately addressed by a visiting child to the grandmother of his companion. | nín hǎo | hāi | huítóu jiàn | wǎn'ān
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of the enquiry chī le ma. | It functions as a customary greeting rather than as an invitation to dine | It constitutes a formal invitation to a banquet to be held that same evening | It is an enquiry regarding the hearer's state of health | It is a valediction employed exclusively at night
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression wǎn'ān? | It is a valediction reserved for bedtime or the conclusion of a late conversation | It is the customary greeting upon arrival at the house of another in the evening hours | It denotes the morning salutation of pupils | It is an expression of gratitude for a meal
exam: Ascertain the phonetic phenomenon whereby the greeting nǐ hǎo is realised as ní hǎo in speech. | A dipping tone preceding another dipping tone is pronounced as a rising tone | The initial consonant is invariably omitted | Interrogative intonation is applied to every greeting | The second syllable is rendered in the neutral tone
